.TH MHEARDD 8 "10 December 2005" Linux "Linux Programmer's Manual"
.SH NAME
mheardd \- collect information about packet activity
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mheardd [-f] [-F] [-l] [-n number] [-p [!]port1[,port2,..]] [-v]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.LP
.B Mheardd
is a daemon that collects the statistics about the activity on all the AX.25
channels that are configured. The list generated is available for viewing by
the
.BR mheard (1)
program. The information collected is a superset of the information normally
collected by similar mheard programs and this is reflected in the options
available for the viewing program. Logging to the system log file may be
enabled which will enable monitoring of pathalogical conditions, for example
invalid frame types and invalid/unknown protocol IDs.
.PP
What goes into the log is who talked to whom, with which control field and
which protocol ID, and never what the frames carried \-\-
so this daemon asks
.BR ax25netd (8)
to leave the information field out of the frames it delivers.  On a band
where most of the traffic is a session rather than a beacon, that is the
majority of the bytes, and none of them reach this daemon.  The addresses, the
control byte and the PID are what every entry is built from and they are all
there.  A
.BR listen (1)
on the same machine is a different client and gets the payloads, so this
costs nothing and is not seen by anything else.
.PP
On a host that also has a kernel AX.25 stack the packet socket is a second
source and the kernel hands over what it assembled, payloads included; there
is nothing to ask it with, and cutting a frame at this end would produce one
that decodes as a different frame.  See
.BR axsock (7).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP 10
.BI \-f
Stay in the foreground: do not fork, and do not detach from the terminal.
This is what a supervisor wants \-\- see
.BR ax25d (8)
for why a unit cannot simply be written as
.B Type=forking
\-\- and it is what
.B \-f
already means in ax25d(8), ax25netd(8) and ax25tcpd(8).
Earlier versions of this program used
.B \-f
for something else: it deleted the mheard logging file at program
startup, which is what
.B \-F
does now.  The foreground switch took the letter so that one option does
not mean two things in one suite, and a script which expects the file to
be deleted has to ask for
.B \-F
as well \-\- mheardd started with
.B \-f
alone keeps the old list and stays in the foreground on top of it.
.TP 10
.BI \-F
Deletes the existing mheard logging file at program startup, this is not the
default.
.TP 10
.BI \-l
Enables logging to the system log file. The default is off.
.TP 10
.BI "\-n number"
Sets the number of entries in the activity list file, the default is 100. The
minimum value allowed is 10 and the maximum is 1000.
.TP 10
.BI "\-p [!]port1[,port2,..]"
With -p, you instruct mheardd to only listen on specified ax25 ports.
You may give more ports, separated by ','.
If you introduce the port(s) by a leading '!', then mheardd will listen
on all ports except those you specified.
.TP 10
.BI \-v
Display the version.
.SH FILES
.LP
@@@AX25_LOCALSTATEDIR@@@/mheard/mheard.dat
.br
@@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/axports
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mheard (1),
.BR ax25 (4).
